rous unless I'm sure I\u2019ll draw another seat/prevalent wind (West Wind = F3). But F2 is South Wind \u2014 not prevalent nor seat wind. So F2 pung wouldn't score much. Better to keep pair than discard one.\n- **Keep W1/W2/W3**: Already a chow, best to keep together.\n\nBest chance at high-scoring hand:\n- If I retain [W1,W2,W3], [W7,W8,W9], and form two more chows + a pair, I can aim for **All Chows** (2 points), possibly **Pure Straight** (16 points) if the other chows fit 4-5-6 and 7-8-9, but I already used 7-8-9.\nWait \u2014 I have two chows: 1-2-3 and 7-8-9 in W. Need middle chow (4-5-6) for Pure Straight. Currently no 4/5/6 in hand. Unlikely.\nBut **Three-Suited Terminal Chows** requires two suits with 123+789 and 55 in third suit \u2014 not applicable.\nAlternatively, **Mixed Straight** (8 points): need 123, 456, 789 in different suits. I have 123 and 789 both in W \u2014 same suit \u2192 invalid for Mixed Straight.\n\nSo Pure Double Chow possible? Yes \u2014 two identical chows. But I don\u2019t have duplicate chows.\n\nAlternative idea: go for **Seven Pairs**? No \u2014 too far from that.\n\nBack to practical: I must discard something safe while preserving potential.\n\nTop candidates to discard:\n- **T1**: Seen twice in game history (across player \u00d72), so only 0 left? Wait: T1 unshown: 2 \u2192 started with 4, I had 1, across discarded 2 \u2192 that\u2019s 3 accounted, one missing. Possibly in next player\u2019s hand. Still, low risk.\n- **T9**: I have one, previous player discarded T9\u00d72, next player \u00d71, across \u00d71 \u2192 total 4 discarded? Wait: starting 4 copies. Previous: T9\u00d72, next: T9\u00d71, across: T9\u00d71 \u2192 total 4! All T9s are discarded! So **T9 cannot be drawn anymore** \u2014 I must be holding the last one.\n\nWait: Unshown T9: 1 \u2192 meaning one still in wall or in someone\u2019s hand.\n\nBut let\u2019s count:\n- Total T9: 4\n- Discarded: previous player: T9\u00d72, next: T9\u00d71, across: T9\u00d71 \u2192 total 4\n\u2192 All four T9s are already discarded! But unshown says T9:1 \u2014 contradiction?\n\nWait: \u201cUnshown tiles\u201d likely means *remaining in wall or concealed*, not including discarded ones. But standard rule: unshown should exclude all revealed tiles.\n\nBut here: \u201cUnshown tiles are: [...] T9: 1\u201d \u2014 despite 4 being played? That can\u2019t be.\n\nCheck game histories:\n- Previous player: Play T9, Play T9 \u2192 2\n- Next player: Play T9 \u2192 1\n- Across: Play T9 \u2192 1\nTotal: 4 \u2192 all out.\n\nTherefore, **T9 count should be 0**, but system says 1 \u2014 possible error, or perhaps one is in my hand?\n\nI have T9:1 in hand \u2192 yes! So total:\n- In play: across:1, next:1, previous:2 \u2192 4\nBut wait \u2014 each player can only discard what they have. Max 4 tiles.\n\nMy hand has one T9 \u2192 that makes 5? Impossible.\n\nAh! Mistake: total number of T9 tiles is 4.\n\nBreakdown:\n- My hand: T9:1\n- Discarded: previous: T9\u00d72, next: T9\u00d71, across: T9\u00d71 \u2192 that\u2019s 4 discarded, plus I hold one \u2192 total 5? Contradiction.\n\nRecheck:\n\nMy hand: \"T1: 1; T9: 1\" \u2192 yes.\n\nGame history:\n- Previous player: Play T9, Play T9 \u2192 2\n- Next player: Play T9 \u2192 1\n- Across: Play T9 \u2192 1\n\nThat\u2019s 4 discarded + 1 in my hand = 5 \u2192 impossible.\n\nTherefore, likely **one of these is misrecorded**.\n\nBut across player\u2019s history: Play T7, T1, ..., Play T1, Play T2, Play B1, Play W1, Play W3 \u2014 only one T9 mentioned? Wait:\n\nAcross player history: ... Play T1, Play W1, Play W3 \u2014 no T9 listed? Let's check:\n\n\"The game history of the player sitting across is: Play T7, Play T1, Chi B4, Play T6, Play T5, Play B7, Peng B2, Play T2, BuGang B2, Play T6, Play T1, Play T2, Play B1, Play W1, Play W3.\"\n\nNo T9 played by across!\n\nPrevious player: \"Play B8, Play B5, Play B5, Play T7, Play J2, Play W9, Play T9, Play B9, Play F1, Play W6, Play T9, Play T7.\" \u2192 T9 played twice.\n\nNext player: \"Play F3, Play B5, Play T6, Play B1, Play B1, Play T8, Play W7, Play B1, Play T8, Play F1.\" \u2192 no T9.\n\nEarlier said \"next player: Play T9\"? No \u2014 mistake in my reading.\n\nSo only **previous player played T9 twice**.\n\nAnd I have one T9.\n\nSo total accounted: 3.\n\nOne T9 unaccounted \u2014 either in wall or in someone\u2019s hand.\n\nUnshown T9:1 \u2192 consistent.\n\nSo T9 is rare but not extinct.\n\nStill, discarding T9 might be risky if someone is waiting on it.\n\nSimilarly, T1: total 4\n- I have 1\n- Across played T1\u00d72\n- Next played B1\u00d73, not T1\n- Previous: no T1\n\u2192 Discarded: 2, in hand:1 \u2192 1 elsewhere.\n\nSafe to discard?\n\nNow, consider scoring:\n\nIf I can complete hand with two chows (already have W1-2-3 and W7-8-9), need two more chows and a pair.\n\nCurrent pair: F2,F2 \u2014 useful.\n\nOther tiles: T1, T9, J3 \u2014 all terminals/honors.\n\nJ3 is White Dragon \u2014 honor.\n\nPossible to form **Outside Hand** (8 points): every meld contains terminals or honors.\n\nMy chows: W1-2-3 and W7-8-9 \u2014 both contain terminals \u2192 qualify.\n\nNeed two more chows that include terminals.\n\nBut T1 needs T2,T3 or T2,T1,T1 \u2014 unlikely.\n\nT9 needs T7,T8 \u2014 T7 played thrice (across\u00d71, prev\u00d72, next\u00d70?), T8 played twice (next\u00d72) \u2014 scarce.\n\nJ3 alone \u2014 can\u2019t form chow.\n\nSo Outside Hand difficult.\n\nAlternatively, **Half Flush** (6 points): all tiles in one suit + honors.\n\nI have mostly W + honors \u2192 possible.\n\nHand: W1, W2, W3, F2, F2, J3, T1, T9, and meld [W7,W8,W9].\n\nIf I discard T1 and T9, I go for **Half Flush**.\n\nRemaining: W1, W2, W3, F2, F2, J3 \u2014 and need to form two more chows in W.\n\nBut I already have two chows \u2014 need two more melds.\n\nCurrently only one pair.\n\nSo I need to draw W4, W5, W6 to form chows.\n\nW5: unshown 1, W6: 2, W4:4 \u2014 W5 very scarce.\n\nDrawing W4 or W6 possible.\n\nBut slow.\n\nBetter to minimize loss and discard a potentially dangerous tile.\n\nNow, look at valid actions: [Play W2; Play W9; Play F2; Play T9; Play T1; Play J3; Play W1; Play W8; Play W7; Play W3]\n\nNote: W7, W8, W9 are in a meld \u2014 already exposed. I can discard W7, W8, W9 individually? Yes \u2014 after forming a chow, you can discard leftover tiles of same suit.\n\nBut W7, W8, W9 are all used in the chow \u2014 I shouldn't have extra copies.\n\nMy hand: W7:1, W8:1, W9:1 \u2014 but meld is [W7,W8,W9] \u2014 so those are separate.\n\nIn Mahjong, when you chi a tile, you place the three tiles down as a meld. The tiles in meld are no longer in hand.\n\nSo my hand should not include W7, W8, W9.\n\nThere is a **critical inconsistency**.\n\nHand: \"W1:1; W2:1; W3:1; W7:1; W8:1; W9:1; ...\" AND meld: [[W7, W8, W9]]\n\nThis implies I have additional W7, W8, W9 in hand beyond the meld \u2014 but each suit has only 4 tiles.\n\nIf meld uses one W7, one W8, one W9, and I hold another W7, W8, W9, that\u2019s two of each \u2014 possible.\n\nYes! I could have two of each.\n\nFor example:\n- Meld: W7, W8, W9 (from chi)\n- Hand: W7, W8, W9 \u2014 extra\n\nSo total: W7\u00d72, W8\u00d72, W9\u00d72 \u2014 possible.\n\nThen valid to discard W7, W8, W9.\n\nBut doing so breaks potential for second chow.\n\nHowever, keeping them risks overconcentration in high numbers.\n\nNow, key point: I already used chi to form [W7,W8,W9], so those are fixed.\n\nThe hand tiles W7, W8, W9 are separate.\n\nBut I also have W1, W2, W3 \u2014 so I could form [W1,W2,W3] as chow.\n\nBut I haven\u2019t declared it yet \u2014 it\u2019s concealed.\n\nSo my concealed chows: [W1,W2,W3] and possibly [W7,W8,W9] again? But I only have one extra W7, W8, W9 \u2014 can\u2019t form another full chow.\n\nUnless I draw duplicates.\n\nBut better to focus on current state.\n\nBest chance: aim for **Two Terminal Chows** (1 point) + **Pure Double Chow** (if two identical chows), but not applicable.\n\nMore importantly: **Seat Wind (West Wind)** is F3. I have F2 (South), not F3.\n\nI have F2 pair \u2014 not useful for Seat or Prevalent Wind.\n\nJ3 is White Dragon \u2014 gives **Dragon Pung** if I get three, but only have one.\n\nSo minimal scoring potential.\n\nPerhaps target **Half Flush** (6 points) + **All Chows** (2 points) + **Self Drawn** (1 point) = 9 points minimum.\n\nBut I need two more chows.\n\nCurrent concealed chow: [W1,W2,W3]\n\nAlready exposed chow: [W7,W8,W9]\n\nNeed two more chows.\n\nAvailable: T1, T9, J3 \u2014 none can form chow easily.\n\nSo must draw W tiles.\n\nBest strategy: discard the most dangerous tile.\n\nT1 and T9 are terminals \u2014 often used in hands.\n\nJ3 is honor \u2014 also useful.\n\nBut look at discards:\n- Across player discarded B3, B4, B5, B2\u00d74 \u2014 focused on B.\n- Previous: B8, B5, T7, W9, T9, B9, F1, W6, T9, T7 \u2014 likes T and B.\n- Next: F3, B5, T6, B1\u00d73, T8, W7, B1, T8, F1 \u2014 many B1s.\n\nB1 is completely discarded (4 times) \u2014 safe.\n\nT1: across discarded twice \u2014 possibly safe.\n\nT9: previous discarded twice \u2014 possibly safe.\n\nBut T9 is terminal \u2014 higher value.\n\nJ3: no one has discarded \u2014 may be safer.\n\nF2: I have a pair \u2014 if I discard one, I break the pair, but F2 is South Wind \u2014 irrelevant.\n\nPrevalent and Seat Wind is West (F3), so F3 is valuable.\n\nF2 has been discarded: next\u00d71, previous\u00d71 \u2192 two out, two left.\n\nPossibly someone waiting on F2.\n\nBut less likely.\n\nBest discard: **T9**\n\nWhy?\n- It\u2019s a terminal, dangerous in general.\n- But previous player discarded it twice \u2014 possibly they have a pair or just clearing.\n- Since I must discard something, and T9 is least likely to help me (no T2/T3/T7/T8 in hand), better to get rid of it.\n\nAlternatively, **J3** \u2014 singleton honor, no support.\n\nBut J3 is White Dragon \u2014 often used in combinations.\n\nHowever, no sign of dragon pungs.\n\nAlso, **T1** is similar.\n\nBut between T1 and T9, T9 is slightly riskier because 8-9-1 or 7-8-9 chows.\n\nT1 can be used in 1-2-3.\n\nBut T2 unshown: 2, T3:4 \u2014 available.\n\nStill, I don\u2019t have T2/T3.\n\nSo neither helps.\n\nNow, another angle: **unseen W5** \u2014 only one left. I previously discarded W5 three times \u2014 so likely safe to assume no one has it.\n\nBut if I keep W1,W2,W3 and W7,W8,W9, I could aim for **Upper Four** or **Lower Four**? No \u2014 split.\n\n**Full Flush**? Only if I drop T and honors.\n\nBut I have T1, T9, F2, F2, J3 \u2014 too many honors.\n\nNot feasible.\n\nConclusion: discard a terminal suited tile.\n\nAmong valid options, **Play T9** is acceptable.\n\nBut also **Play T1**.\n\nWhich is safer?\n\nT1: across discarded twice \u2014 possibly they completed a chow and discarding extra.\n\nT9: previous discarded twice \u2014 same logic.\n\nBut previous player also discarded W9, B9 \u2014 seems to be dumping terminals.\n\nSo T9 may be safe.\n\nSimilarly, T1.\n\nBut I have both \u2014 must keep one as potential part of chow? No \u2014 no supporting tiles.\n\nSo either is discardable.\n\nBut note: **Chi W8** was in my history \u2014 I formed [W7,W8,W9] using a discarded W8.\n\nSo that action is past.\n\nNow, looking at valid actions: **Play T9** is available.\n\nAlso **Play T1**.\n\nBut let's see which one preserves better shape.\n\nIf I keep T1 and T9, I have two terminals \u2014 bad.\n\nMust discard one.\n\nSlight preference: discard **T9**, because:\n- T9 is higher number, often more dangerous (used in 789 chows)\n- T1 can be used in 123, more common\n- But I have no T2/T3, so irrelevant\n- No strong reason\n\nAlternatively, discard **J3** \u2014 singleton honor, less likely to complete a pung for others.\n\nBut J3 is White Dragon \u2014 sometimes kept for scoring.\n\nBut no one has discarded J3 \u2014 possibly safe.\n\nBut F2 has been discarded \u2014 more likely to be in others\u2019 hands.\n\nActually, safest tile to discard is one that has been frequently discarded.\n\nF2: next\u00d71, previous\u00d71 \u2192 only two out.\n\nT1: across\u00d72\n\nT9: previous\u00d72\n\nSo T1 and T9 have been played twice each \u2014 safer.\n\nBetween them, **Play T9** is fine.\n\nBut wait \u2014 **Play W2**? That would break my concealed [W1,W2,W3] chow.\n\nVery bad \u2014 that chow is valuable for **All Chows** and possible **Pure Straight** component.\n\nSimilarly, W1 or W3 \u2014 same.\n\nSo do NOT discard W1, W2, W3.\n\nDo NOT discard W7, W8, W9 \u2014 I might use them for second chow if I draw matching tiles.\n\nBut currently, better to keep them.\n\nF2: I have a pair \u2014 breaking it hurts potential for **Pung of Winds** (but not relevant).\n\nSo best to discard **T9** or **T1** or **J3**.\n\nFinal choice: **Play T9**\n\nBecause:\n- It\u2019s a terminal\n- Less useful to me\n- Frequently discarded\n\nReason: Preserves the concealed chow [W1,W2,W3] and the pair F2, while removing a potentially dangerous terminal tile (T9) that is difficult to integrate into a meld. This improves hand efficiency and safety.\n\nAnswer: Play T9"}
