n \u2014 possible danger, but dragons are often risky to keep.\n\nHowever, **J3** is least connected to any potential pattern in your hand, and cannot form a pair or pung without two more copies (unlikely). So best to discard **J3**.\n\nReason: Discarding J3 removes a useless honor tile that blocks hand progression. It does not contribute to any potential scoring pattern in your current configuration. Keeping W1, W2, W3 together preserves a concealed chow for All Chows and Half Flush. F2 pair remains for potential pair use. This improves chances to reach 8+ points via Half Flush (6) + All Chows (2) + possibly Pure Double Chow (1) or Short Straight (2) depending on final shape.\n\nAnswer: Play J3"}
