ely to be wanted for chows if others are collecting.\n\nUnshown W2:4 \u2014 all four still potentially available, so no one has discarded much.\n\nBut no one has played W2 in history.\n\nAcross played W1,W3, not W2.\n\nNext player no W2.\n\nPrevious no.\n\nMe no.\n\nSo W2 is very live.\n\nDangerous to discard.\n\nSimilarly, W3: across played W3, so one out.\n\nI have one.\n\nUnshown:3.\n\nLess live than W2.\n\nW1: across played W1, so one out.\n\nI have one.\n\nUnshown:3.\n\nSame.\n\nW7: next player played W7, so one out.\n\nI have one.\n\nUnshown:2.\n\nStill live.\n\nBut perhaps the safest is to discard a terminal like W9.\n\nOr T1.\n\nT1: across played three times? No, twice.\n\nAnd I have one.\n\nSo three out, one unshown.\n\nSo very few left, so discarding T1 is relatively safe.\n\nSimilarly, B9: previous played B9, across played B9, so two out, unshown:1, so low.\n\nBut T1 is in my hand.\n\nSo T1 has only one left unseen, so discarding it is safe.\n\nWhereas W2 has four unshown, so very dangerous.\n\nSo best to discard a tile with few copies left.\n\nT1: only one other unshown (since two discarded, one in hand, total four, unshown:2 \u2014 wait, unshown:2 means two not revealed, so if two discarded, two unshown, I have one, so one of the unshown is mine, one is truly hidden.\n\nSo one other T1 exists.\n\nSimilarly for others.\n\nBut T1 has been played twice by across, so known.\n\nSo opponents may be avoiding it.\n\nSo safer to discard.\n\nSimilarly, W5:0 unshown, but I'm not holding it.\n\nFor discard, T1 is a good candidate.\n\nAlso, it's in a different suit, so less central.\n\nW1,W2,W3 are in the same suit, keeping them might allow a flush attempt, but with terminals and honors, not pure.\n\nBut Half Flush with characters and honors is possible, worth 6 points.\n\nCurrently I have many character tiles.\n\nBut I have T1 and T3, which are bamboo, so mixed suit.\n\nSo not Half Flush unless I discard bamboos.\n\nBut I have T1 and T3\u00d72 \u2014 three bamboo tiles.\n\nIf I discard T1, I still have T3\u00d72.\n\nIf I discard both T1 and T3, I can go for Half Flush.\n\nBut I have to keep F2\u00d72, which is honor.\n\nHalf Flush allows honors.\n\nHalf Flush: only one suit of suited tiles and honors.\n\nI have character and bamboo \u2014 two suits, so currently not eligible.\n\nTo achieve Half Flush, I must discard all bamboo or all character.\n\nBut I have many character tiles: W1,W2,W3,W7,W8,W9 \u2014 six character, and T1,T3,T3 \u2014 three bamboo.\n\nSo better to keep character and discard bamboo.\n\nSo I should discard T1 or T3.\n\nBut T3\u00d72 \u2014 if I discard one T3, I break the pair.\n\nIf I discard T1, I keep T3\u00d72, but still have bamboo.\n\nTo eliminate bamboo, I need to discard all T1 and T3.\n\nBut I can only discard one at a time.\n\nEventually, I might draw another bamboo, but unlikely.\n\nSo long-term, aim for Half Flush by discarding bamboo tiles.\n\nCurrently, I can discard T1.\n\nThen later, if I draw a bad tile, discard T3.\n\nBut T3\u00d72 is a good pair for Seven Pairs.\n\nHalf Flush is only 6 points, while Seven Pairs is 24.\n\nSo Seven Pairs is better.\n\nMoreover, with F2\u00d72, T3\u00d72, and potential for more pairs, Seven Pairs is achievable.\n\nWhereas Half Flush would require giving up the pair.\n\nSo better to pursue Seven Pairs.\n\nTherefore, keep all paired tiles, and discard a singleton terminal.\n\nAmong the choices, T1 is a good discard because:\n- It's a terminal\n- Bamboo suit, so not helping character flush\n- Has been played twice, so somewhat safe\n- Only one other copy out there\n\nWhereas discarding W1,W2,W3 might break a potential shape, but not really.\n\nW2 is not terminal, so discarding W2 loses a simple tile, which might be more useful.\n\nBut for Seven Pairs, all tiles are equal.\n\nSo discard T1.\n\nIt's in the valid actions: Play T1.\n\nOther option: Play W9, also terminal.\n\nBut W9 is in my main suit.\n\nNo strong reason.\n\nI think T1 is fine.\n\nFinal decision: Play T1.\n\nEvaluation: The hand has potential for Seven Pairs (24 points) with existing pairs F2\u00d72 and T3\u00d72. Discarding a terminal tile from a less dominant suit (Bamboo) reduces risk and streamlines the hand. T1 is a suitable discard as it is a terminal, has been frequently discarded by opponents (reducing its danger), and keeps the focus on building pairs within the more abundant Character suit.\n\nReason: To pursue a high-scoring Seven Pairs pattern, preserve existing pairs (F2 and T3), and safely discard a less useful terminal tile (T1) that belongs to a minority suit and has limited winning potential.\n\nAnswer: Play T1"}
