lso gone. So only possible chow with W4 is [W4,W5,W6] \u2014 impossible. So W4 is dead.\n\nSimilarly, W1 can only form [W1,W2,W3] \u2014 I have W1,W2,W3 \u2014 perfect! So I can form [W1,W2,W3] if I keep them.\n\nW2 and W3 also support [W2,W3,W4] \u2014 but W4 is weak since W5/W6 gone.\n\nSo best sequence is [W1,W2,W3].\n\nI also have W4 alone \u2014 no good extension.\n\nW7, W8, W9 in hand \u2014 can form [W7,W8,W9] again? But I already have one such chow. Having two identical chows could help Pure Triple Chow (24 points) or Quadruple Chow (48) if four of them, but unlikely.\n\nCurrently I have one chow [W7,W8,W9], and hold W7,W8,W9 \u2014 so if I draw or claim another W7,W8,W9, I could pong or build another chow.\n\nBut better to aim for [W1,W2,W3] as second chow.\n\nT3\u00d72 is a pair candidate.\n\nHonors: F2 and J1 \u2014 singletons. F2 is South Wind, not prevalent (West) or seat (West) \u2014 so not useful. J1 is Red Dragon \u2014 could form pung for 2 points (Dragon Pung), but only one seen.\n\nAcross has B2 kong, B3B4B5 chow \u2014 so focusing on Bams.\n\nPrevious player has Peng J3 \u2014 so collecting dragons.\n\nNext player has no melds \u2014 might be waiting.\n\nUnshown J1: 4 \u2014 all still available? Total 4 copies. I have one, so 3 unshown \u2014 possible.\n\nBut risky to keep J1.\n\nF2: 3 unshown \u2014 possible, but wind not favorable.\n\nBest path: go for **All Chows** (2 points) or **Pure Straight** (16 points) if I can get [W1,W2,W3], [W4,W5,W6] (impossible), or [W3,W4,W5] (W5 gone).\n\nNo \u2014 W5 and W6 are exhausted \u2014 cannot form middle chows.\n\nThus, **Pure Straight** impossible.\n\n**Mixed Triple Chow** requires three chows same numbers different suits \u2014 I have only Characters.\n\n**Quadruple Chow** \u2014 need four identical chows \u2014 very hard.\n\nBetter to aim for **Seven Pairs**? I have T3\u00d72 \u2014 one pair. If I keep pairs, but others are singles.\n\nOr **Chicken Hand** \u2014 minimal 8-point hand.\n\nCurrently, what can I score?\n\nIf I complete [W1,W2,W3], I have two chows in same suit: [W1,W2,W3] and [W7,W8,W9] \u2014 that gives **Short Straight** (1 point) if adjacent, but they are not \u2014 gap at 4,5,6.\n\nNot consecutive.\n\nBut **Two Terminal Chows** \u2014 chow of 1,2,3 and 7,8,9 in same suit \u2014 worth 1 point (if pure) or part of higher patterns.\n\nHere, both in W suit \u2014 so **Two Terminal Chows** = 1 point.\n\nAlso, if I have three chows in same suit, could get **Pure Shifted Chows** (16 pts) if like 123,234,345 \u2014 but I can\u2019t due to W5/W6 absence.\n\nAlternative: discard honors to streamline.\n\nBest discards: F2 or J1 \u2014 honor singles, no immediate value.\n\nT3\u00d72 \u2014 I could keep as pair.\n\nBut if I discard one T3, I break the pair \u2014 worse.\n\nKeep T3 pair.\n\nW4 is dead \u2014 cannot form any chow \u2014 because needs W5 and W6, both gone.\n\nSo W4 is useless.\n\nSimilarly, W7, W8, W9 \u2014 I already have a chow of them. Holding extra ones might allow a pair or pung, but low probability.\n\nBut W7: 2 unshown \u2014 I have one, so 3 accounted for? Total 4. Where is the fourth? Possibly discarded.\n\nBut safer to keep W7-W9 for potential pungs or another chow.\n\nBut W4 has no future.\n\nSo best discard: **Play W4**\n\nOther options:\n- Play F2: safe, honor\n- Play J1: riskier, dragon, previous player has J3 pung, might collect dragons\n- Play W1: breaks [W1,W2,W3] possibility\n- Similarly W2, W3: bad\n\nW7, W8, W9: I can afford to discard one if needed, but better to keep for flexibility.\n\nBut W4 is definitely dead.\n\nAlso, **One Voided Suit**: if I can eliminate Dots (B), I have no B in hand, and across has B\u2019s, but I have none \u2014 so currently only W and T and honors \u2014 three suits? W (Characters), T (Bamboos), F/J (Honors) \u2014 so three types.\n\nBut if I discard W4, still have W and T.\n\nTo get **One Voided Suit**, I need to drop one suit entirely.\n\nCurrently, I have W and T. T only has T3\u00d72.\n\nIf I discard both T3, I lose pair, but could go pure W.\n\nBut T3 pair is useful.\n\nAlternatively, keep T3 as pair, focus on W chows.\n\nBut with W5/W6 gone, hard to complete middle.\n\nBest chance: go for **Outside Hand** (8 points) \u2014 all melds contain terminals or honors.\n\nMy current meld [W7,W8,W9] \u2014 W7 and W9 are terminals? W7 is not terminal \u2014 terminal is 1 or 9.\n\nW7 is middle.\n\nOnly W9 is terminal.\n\nSo [W7,W8,W9] has only one terminal \u2014 not sufficient for Outside Hand.\n\n**All Chows** (2 points) \u2014 if I have four chows and a pair.\n\nI have one chow, need three more.\n\nPossible if I form [W1,W2,W3], and maybe [W2,W3,W4] \u2014 but W4 is there, W5 gone \u2014 no.\n\n[W3,W4,W5] impossible.\n\nSo maximum two chows in W: [W1,W2,W3] and [W7,W8,W9].\n\nThen I have T3 pair \u2014 so one pair.\n\nNeed two more chows \u2014 but no other tiles.\n\nUnless I use honors \u2014 but honors can't form chows.\n\nSo impossible to get four chows.\n\nThus, **All Chows** unreachable.\n\nSimilarly, **Half Flush** (6 points): one suit and honors.\n\nI have W and T and F and J \u2014 multiple suits.\n\nIf I discard T3, I eliminate T suit.\n\nThen hand becomes W and honors \u2014 qualifies for **Half Flush** (6 points).\n\nAnd if I form [W1,W2,W3], I have two chows in W.\n\nStill need more.\n\nBut Half Flush is 6 points \u2014 need 2 more for 8.\n\nPossible with **Seat Wind** \u2014 but I don\u2019t have F3 (West Wind) \u2014 I have F2 (South).\n\nNo.\n\n**Dragon Pung** \u2014 I have only one J1.\n\nNo.\n\n**Pung of Terminals or Honors** \u2014 if I had a pung of W9 or something, but not.\n\nCurrently, best I can hope for is **Chicken Hand** \u2014 8 points by default if nothing else.\n\nBut Chicken Hand is worth 8 points only if no other pattern applies \u2014 but actually, per rules, \"Chicken Hand\" is a specific 8-point pattern defined as a hand with no other scoring elements (except perhaps flowers, ignored).\n\nBut in practice, if I have no pattern, I can't win.\n\nBut the pattern exists.\n\nBut better to try to score minimally.\n\nBack to W4 \u2014 it's dead.\n\nDiscard W4 to commit to high or low.\n\nKeep W1,W2,W3 for [W1,W2,W3].\n\nKeep W7,W8,W9 for potential pung or pair.\n\nT3 pair.\n\nDiscard F2 or J1 or W4.\n\nF2: South Wind \u2014 not useful.\n\nJ1: Dragon \u2014 might be wanted.\n\nW4: completely dead.\n\nSo safest discard: **Play W4**\n\nIt improves hand efficiency.\n\nReason: W4 cannot form any Chow due to W5 and W6 being exhausted (0 unshown), making it a stranded tile with no prospects. Retaining it blocks the development of a more coherent hand. Discarding it streamlines the hand toward potential patterns involving the [W1,W2,W3] Chow and existing [W7,W8,W9], while preserving the T3 pair and reducing reliance on useless tiles.\n\nAnswer: Play W4"}
