er W7 later \u2192 possible to hold W7:1 now.\n\nSame for W8:\n- You claimed W8 in Chi \u2192 one used\n- You played no W8\n- Others: you played W8? No. Previous player played W8? No. Across? No. Next? Yes \u2014 played W7, not W8.\nWait \u2014 no one else played W8 except in your Chi.\nBut unshown W8: 3 \u2192 three remain? Total 4, so only one exposed (your Chi)\nBut you used one in meld, so one exposed \u2192 three unshown \u2192 ok.\nSo if you drew a W8 later, you could hold it.\n\nBut after Chi, you never played W8 \u2014 and hand shows W8:1 \u2192 possible.\n\nSimilarly W9:\n- You used one in meld\n- You played B9, not W9\n- History: you played W9? No. Others: previous player played W9 \u2192 one exposed\nSo total exposed: 2 (your meld + their discard)\nUnshown: 3 \u2192 wait, unshown W9: 3? But total 4, 2 exposed \u2192 should be 2 unshown \u2192 discrepancy.\n\nWait: unshown W9: 3 \u2014 but that can't be.\n\nList:\nTotal W9: 4\nExposed:\n- Your meld: used one W9\n- Previous player: played W9 (in history: Play W9)\n\u2192 2 exposed\nSo 2 left in play\nBut unshown says W9: 3 \u2192 contradiction.\n\nAh! Likely the \"unshown\" list means **remaining unseen tiles in wall and opponents' hands**, i.e., **not yet discarded or melded visibly**.\n\nBut when you form a meld via Chi, the tiles are visible on table \u2014 so **W7, W8, W9 in your meld are now exposed**, so they are **not unshown**.\n\nSo unshown counts should exclude:\n- All discarded tiles\n- All melded tiles (even if formed by Chi)\n\nSo for W9:\n- Total: 4\n- Used in your meld: 1\n- Discarded by previous player: 1 (Play W9)\n- You played W9? No\n- Others? Across played B9, T9; next played T9; you played B9\nNo other W9 seen\nSo 2 exposed \u2192 2 unshown \u2192 but listed as 3 \u2192 error?\n\nWait, unshown W9: 3 \u2014 likely typo or miscount.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the \"unshown\" includes only wall, not hidden in hands? No, standard is \"unseen\" = not revealed.\n\nGiven confusion, better to trust the **hand composition** provided: you currently hold W1, W2, W3, W4, W7, W8, W9 (one each), T3\u00d72, F2, J1.\n\nAnd you have a meld [W7, W8, W9] \u2014 so you must have **at least two W7, two W8, two W9**? But hand says count 1 each.\n\nThis is inconsistent.\n\nUnless the hand list is **current hand only**, and melds are separate.\n\nYes \u2014 correct interpretation:\n- Your **hand tiles** are what you currently hold.\n- Your **melds** are exposed groups.\n- So if you have meld [W7,W8,W9], and hand shows W7:1, then you have **a second W7 in hand**.\n\nTherefore, you must have **duplicates**:\n- You used one W7 in meld, and hold another \u2192 total at least two W7\n- Same for W8 and W9\n\nBut total per tile is 4.\n\nFor W7:\n- You used one in meld\n- You hold one \u2192 2 accounted\n- Unshown: 2 \u2192 ok\nFor W8:\n- You used one in meld (claimed via Chi)\n- You hold one \u2192 2\n- Unshown: 3 \u2192 implies only one exposed? But your meld exposes one \u2192 should be 3 unshown \u2192 ok if total 4\nBut 1 (meld) + 1 (in your hand) = 2 \u2192 2 unshown \u2192 but listed as 3 \u2192 contradiction.\n\nWait: \"unshown\" = number of tiles not yet seen by anyone (still in wall or unknown hands)\n\nSo:\n- For W8:\n- Total: 4\n- Exposed: your meld shows one W8 (claimed), and no other W8 discarded (check history):\n  - You: no Play W8\n  - Next: no\n  - Across: no\n  - Previous: no\n\u2192 Only one W8 exposed\n- So 3 unshown \u2192 matches\n- You hold one W8 in hand \u2192 that one is \"unshown\" until played\n- So \"unshown\" includes tiles in players' hands and wall\n\nYes \u2014 so unshown counts include **all tiles not yet discarded or melded publicly**.\n\nSo you holding W8 is part of \"unshown\".\n\nSo no contradiction.\n\nBack to evaluation.\n\nYou hold:\n- W1, W2, W3, W4, W7, W8, W9 (one each) \u2014 but W7, W8, W9 are also in your meld \u2192 so you have **two copies each of W7, W8, W9**\n- That means you have **concealed pairs**: W7, W8, W9 \u2014 almost a concealed kong base\n\nMoreover:\n- You have T3\u00d72 \u2192 pair\n- F2:1, J1:1\n\nSo your hand has:\n- Three concealed pairs: W7, W8, W9 (each paired)\n- One pair: T3\u00d72\n- And W1, W2, W3, W4, F2, J1 as singles\n\nWait \u2014 you can't have W7 as single if you have two \u2014 so W7:2 \u2192 one used in meld, one in hand \u2192 so in hand: one W7 \u2192 forms a pair only if you have two, but hand says W7:1 \u2192 so not a pair.\n\nHand composition: [W1:1; W2:1; W3:1; W4:1; W7:1; W8:1; W9:1; T3:2; F2:1; J1:1]\n\nAnd meld: [W7, W8, W9] \u2014 formed by Chi, so you contributed one W7, one W9, and claimed W8.\n\nSo pre-Chi, you had at least W7\u22651, W9\u22651\nAfter Chi, you lost one W7, one W9\nNow you have in hand: W7:1 \u2192 so you must have drawn another W7 later\nSimilarly, W8:1 in hand \u2014 you didn't have it before (since you claimed it), so you drew it\nSame for W9: you used one, now hold one \u2192 drew another\n\nSo you have:\n- After drawing: W7, W8, W9 each in hand as singles\n- Plus you have T3\u00d72\n\nSo no pairs currently \u2014 all are singles except T3\n\nBut you could aim for **Seven Pairs**, but only one pair so far.\n\nBetter strategy: **build on Characters**\n\nYou have W1, W2, W3, W4, and you have W7, W8, W9 in hand and meld.\n\nIf you keep W1, W2, W3, you can try to draw W2 or W1 to make a pair or chow.\n\nBut more promising: **Mixed Triple Chow** or **Mixed Shifted Pungs**, but hard.\n\nBest scoring chance: **Half Flush** (8 pts) \u2014 all honors and one suit. You're mostly in Characters, with some honors.\n\nOr **All Types** (8 pts): you have W (Characters), T (Bamboos: T3), B (Dots: via across's meld), F (winds), J (dragons) \u2014 yes, you have all types represented.\n\nBut you need to keep a diverse hand.\n\nHowever, **T3 is your only Bamboo**, and you have two \u2014 if you discard one, you still have T3.\n\nBut if you discard T3, you lose bamboo presence.\n\nBut Half Flush gives 8 pts, while All Types gives 8 \u2014 similar.\n\nBut Half Flush requires **only one suit and honors** \u2014 you have T3, which is Bamboo \u2014 so if you keep it, you break Half Flush.\n\nAh! Critical point.\n\nTo qualify for **Half Flush**, you must have **only one suited suit (plus honors)**.\n\nCurrently, you have:\n- Characters (W): many\n- Bamboos (T): T3\u00d72\n- Honors: F2, J1\n\nSo you have **two suits** \u2014 W and T \u2192 not Half Flush.\n\nIf you **discard both T3s**, you will have only W and honors \u2192 eligible for Half Flush.\n\nSimilarly, **All Simples** requires no terminals or honors \u2014 you have many terminals (W1, W9, etc.) and honors \u2014 no.\n\n**Outside Hand** (8 pts): every meld and pair contains terminals or honors \u2014 possible.\n\nBut let's assess discards.\n\nBest move: **discard T3** to commit to **Half Flush**.\n\nBecause:\n- You have strong Character base: W1, W2, W3, W4, W7, W8, W9\n- You can aim to form more Character melds\n- With honors, you can complete Half Flush (8 pts)\n- Additional patterns: Dragon Pung (if you get more J1), Seat Wind (but F3, not F2), Prevalent Wind same.\n\nJ1: unshown 3 \u2192 plenty available.\n\nF2: unshown 3, you have one, others may want.\n\nBut T3: only two left unshown (total 4, you have 2, none seen) \u2192 so possibly safe.\n\nDiscarding T3 allows you to pursue Half Flush.\n\nOther options:\n- Discard F2: South Wind, not prevalent or seat wind (West=F3), so low value. But it's an honor \u2014 needed for balance.\n- Discard J1: Dragon \u2014 risky, others may be collecting.\n- Discard W1/W2/W3/W4: breaking potential chows. W1-W2-W3 is possible \u2014 you have W1,W2,W3 \u2192 if you draw W2, you can have a pair or even two chows.\n\nBut W4 is less connected \u2014 no W5 or W6.\n\nSo **W4 is the weakest Character tile**.\n\nBut if you discard W4, you still keep W1-W2-W3 potential.\n\nAlternatively, **discard F2** \u2014 it's a wind, but not valuable to you.\n\nBut for Half Flush, keeping one honor is fine.\n\nBest choice: **discard T3** to eliminate the second suit and enable **Half Flush** (8 pts).\n\nEven if you don't complete it, you can still win with other combinations.\n\nYou have two T3s \u2014 discard one.\n\nValid action: **Play T3**\n\nNow, check if any other action enables faster win.\n\nDo you have any ready waits?\n\nNot really \u2014 no clear pair or wait.\n\nBut you have multiple possibilities.\n\nAnother angle: **Robbing the kong** \u2014 but no one is konging.\n\nOr **Last Tile** \u2014 not there yet.\n\nConclusion: To improve hand structure and aim for **Half Flush (8 pts)**, remove the Bamboo suit by discarding T3.\n\nReason: Eliminating the T3 reduces the number of suits in your hand, enabling eligibility for the 8-point **Half Flush** pattern, which requires only one suit and honors. Keeping two different suited suits blocks this valuable pattern.\n\nAnswer: Play T3"}
