24 pts): only 1,2,3 \u2014 you have W1,W2,W3, but also W4,W7,W8,W9 \u2014 too high.\n- **All Chows** (2 pts): possible if all melds are chows.\n- **Outside Hand** (4 pts): every group has terminals or honors \u2014 but W1-2-3 is inside, so conflicts.\n\nBest shot: **Full Flush** (24) + **All Chows** (2) + possibly **Two Concealed Pungs** if you form pairs, but you have no pungs.\n\nAlternatively, **Pure Double Chow** (1 pt) if you get two identical chows.\n\nBut with W5 gone, hard to complete middle sequences.\n\nHowever, you have W1, W2, W3, W4 \u2014 can form:\n- W1-2-3 and W2-3-4 (overlap)\n- Or W2-3-4 and need W5 for W4-5-6, but W5 gone.\n\nSo best is to aim for:\n- W1-2-3 (chow)\n- W7-8-9 (already have, and another copy)\n- W7, W8, W9 in hand can form another chow or pair\n\nBut you can only use each tile once.\n\nCurrent plan:\n- Keep W1, W2, W3 for W1-2-3 chow\n- Keep W7, W8, W9 for another chow or pair\n- W4 is awkward \u2014 can only be used in W4-5-6, but W5 gone \u2192 useless\n- T2, T3: can form T2-3-4 chow, but T4 is rare (only 2 left), and across player already Peng T3, so dangerous\n- F2: single wind \u2014 not useful unless pair, but F2 unshown: 3 left, so possible, but not helpful for score\n- J1: dragon, single \u2014 same\n\nThus, **W4, T2, T3, F2, J1 are weak**\n\nAmong valid plays: Play W4, T2, T3, F2, J1, W1, W2, W3, W7, W8, W9\n\nBest discard:\n- **Play W4**: W5 gone, so W4 cannot form W4-5-6. Only hope is W2-3-4 or W3-4-5, but W5 gone \u2192 W3-4-5 impossible. W2-3-4 possible. But you have W2, W3 \u2014 so W4 could help. But if you keep W1, better to keep W1-2-3. W2-3-4 requires W4, but then you'd need to drop W1. Trade-off.\n\nCompare:\n- Option A: Keep W1-2-3, discard W4 \u2192 solid chow, good for Full Flush\n- Option B: Keep W2-3-4, discard W1 \u2192 same value, but W1 is more common in early sequences\n\nBut both are equal. However, W1 is terminal, so slightly less flexible.\n\nBut in terms of future draws:\n- Drawing W5: 0 left \u2014 impossible\n- W6: 0 left \u2014 also gone\n- W4: 3 unshown \u2014 possible\n- W3: 3 unshown \u2014 possible\n- W2: 4 unshown \u2014 plenty\n- W1: 3 unshown \u2014 possible\n\nSo W4 is safer to keep than W1? No \u2014 W1 can only be used in W1-2-3, W4 in W2-3-4 or W4-5-6 (blocked).\n\nSince W5 and W6 are gone, **W4 is dead tile** \u2014 cannot form any chow involving W4 unless W2-3-4, but you\u2019d need to draw W2 or W3, which you already have.\n\nYou already have W2, W3 \u2014 so if you keep W4, you can form W2-3-4 chow.\n\nSimilarly, with W1, you can form W1-2-3.\n\nBut you can\u2019t form both.\n\nYou have W1, W2, W3, W4 \u2014 so you can form either:\n- W1-2-3 + leave W4 (useless later)\n- W2-3-4 + leave W1 (terminal, might pair?)\n\nBut W1 is a terminal \u2014 can be part of Outside Hand, but not helpful alone.\n\nBetter to keep **W1-2-3** because:\n- It's a standard starting chow\n- W4 is harder to use due to missing W5/W6\n\nMoreover, you have another W7, W8, W9 \u2014 can form a second chow or wait for pair.\n\nF2 and J1 are honors \u2014 keeping both breaks Full Flush.\n\nT2 and T3 can form T2-3-4, but T4 is scarce (only 2 left), and across player has already collected T3\u00d73 and discarded T2, T1, etc. \u2014 likely has completed T3 chow, so T2-3-4 risky.\n\nThus, safest to discard **non-Character tiles first**.\n\nAmong F2, J1, T2, T3 \u2014 all are bad for Full Flush.\n\nDiscard one.\n\nBut valid actions include Play F2, Play J1, Play T2, Play T3.\n\nAlso Play W4 \u2014 which is Character, but weak.\n\nBest discard: **Play J1** or **Play F2** or **Play T2/T3**\n\nBut J1: Dragon tile \u2014 if you could form pung, worth points, but only 3 unshown, and no sign of collecting.\n\nSimilarly, F2: not prevalent or seat wind (West is F3), so not valuable.\n\nT2 and T3: part of Bamboo suit, but across player has [T3\u00d73], so T3 is dangerous \u2014 might win on T3.\n\nT2 is safer.\n\nBut better to remove non-Character tiles to commit to Full Flush.\n\nSo discard J1, F2, T2, T3 \u2014 pick one.\n\nWhich one is least useful?\n\n- J1: single dragon, no pung \u2014 no point\n- F2: single wind, not relevant \u2014 no point\n- T2: could be used in T2-3-4, but T4 rare\n- T3: already has 3 exposed by across \u2014 very dangerous to keep, risk of discard being winning tile\n\nSo **T3 is dangerous** \u2014 never discard T3 if someone might be waiting on it.\n\nBut you are discarding, so avoid playing T3.\n\nSimilarly, J1: 3 unshown \u2014 possible someone wants, but no sign.\n\nF2: 3 unshown \u2014 F1 and F3 are more popular, but F2 less so.\n\nBest discard: **J1** \u2014 it's a dragon, but you're not building dragons, and it breaks the flush.\n\nAlternatively, **F2** \u2014 same logic.\n\nBut let's see scoring potential.\n\nIf you go for Full Flush, you need all melds in Characters.\n\nCurrent meld: [W7,W8,W9] \u2014 good.\n\nConcealed: aim for W1-2-3, and maybe W7-8-9 again, or W2-3-4, etc.\n\nBut with W4 and no W5, best is W1-2-3 and W7-8-9 (second copy).\n\nThen pair: could be W7, W8, or W9, or any other Character.\n\nYou have extra W7, W8, W9 \u2014 so can make pair.\n\nThus, hand like: [W7,W8,W9] (exposed), [W1,W2,W3] (concealed), [W7,W8,W9] (concealed chow), and pair W2 say \u2014 but only one W2.\n\nWait \u2014 you have only one W1, W2, W3, W4.\n\nSo cannot form two chows using W1-2-3 and W2-3-4.\n\nMust choose.\n\nBetter: form W1-2-3, and W7-8-9 (using hand copy), and pair from W7, W8, or W9.\n\nYou have W7, W8, W9 in hand \u2014 so if you form chow with them, no pair.\n\nNeed a fifth W7 for pair? No \u2014 only four copies.\n\nYou have:\n- Meld: W7, W8, W9\n- Hand: W7, W8, W9\n\nSo total: two W7, two W8, two W9\n\nPerfect \u2014 can form:\n- Meld: W7,W8,W9 (already)\n- Concealed: W7,W8,W9 (chow)\n- Pair: W7, W8, or W9\n\nYes! So you can have two identical chows: W7-8-9 \u00d72, and pair of W7 for example.\n\nBut you need to form the second chow.\n\nCurrently, the second W7,W8,W9 are in hand \u2014 so you can use them as-is.\n\nBut you also have W1,W2,W3,W4,T2,T3,F2,J1.\n\nTo complete hand, you need one more chow or pung.\n\nOptions:\n- Form W1-2-3 chow\n- Then pair with, say, W4? But only one W4\n- Or pair with T2? But T2 single\n\nSo better to form W1-2-3 and use one of the W7/W8/W9 as pair.\n\nYes.\n\nSo required: keep W1,W2,W3,W7,W8,W9 (extra set)\n\nBut you have only one set of W7,W8,W9 in hand \u2014 so must keep them.\n\nW4, T2, T3, F2, J1 are surplus.\n\nMust discard one now.\n\nBest discard: **J1** \u2014 it's a dragon, but not helping, and you're going for Full Flush.\n\nSimilarly, F2, T2, T3 are also bad.\n\nBut J1 is a honor tile, and you already have no honor melds, so discard early.\n\nAlso, previous player Peng J3 and F4 \u2014 showing interest in honors, but not J1 specifically.\n\nJ1 has 3 unshown \u2014 relatively safe.\n\nT3 is very dangerous \u2014 across player has Peng T3, so likely completing hand around T3 \u2014 do NOT discard T3.\n\nSimilarly, T2 might be dangerous.\n\nF2: previous player discarded F1, F3, F4 \u2014 seems to be clearing winds, so F2 might be safe.\n\nW4: harmless, but Character, so better to keep than non-suit.\n\nSo best discard: **J1**\n\nIt\u2019s a single honor, breaks the flush, no chance of pung.\n\nReason: To pursue Full Flush (24 points), I must eliminate non-Character tiles. J1 is a standalone Dragon tile with no pung, and keeping it prevents achieving Full Flush. Discarding it commits to a high-value all-Character hand, leveraging my strong W1-2-3 and double W7-8-9 potential.\n\nAnswer: Play J1"}
