istent strategy.\n\nBut **T2 is just as bad**.\n\nWait \u2014 look at the across player: they have [T3,T3,T3] \u2014 they punged T3.\n\nThat means **T3 is fully exposed (0 left)** \u2014 and they likely have no need for T2 or T4.\n\nBut they completed T3 pung \u2014 so T2 and T4 are safe against them.\n\nPrevious player has no T melds.\n\nNext player hasn\u2019t shown interest.\n\nSo T2 and T4 are safe.\n\nBut **T2 forms a sequence with W2? No, different suits**.\n\nNo benefit.\n\nHowever, consider **scoring patterns**.\n\nIf you go for **All Chows** (2 points), you need four chows and a pair.\n\nYou already have one chow: [W7,W8,W9].\n\nIf you can form [W1,W2,W3], that\u2019s two.\n\nThen you need two more chows.\n\nYour remaining tiles: T2, T4, F1, F2 \u2014 all useless for chows.\n\nSo unless you draw favorable tiles, **All Chows** is unlikely.\n\nAlternatively, **Outside Hand** (8 points): every meld and pair contains terminals or honors.\n\nYour current meld [W7,W8,W9] qualifies (W9 is terminal).\n\nIf you make [W1,W2,W3], W1 is terminal \u2014 qualifies.\n\nBut T2 and T4 are simples \u2014 not terminals.\n\nF1 and F2 are honors \u2014 okay.\n\nSo to qualify for **Outside Hand**, you must not have T2 or T4 in final hand.\n\nTherefore, you must discard them anyway.\n\nSo better to discard **T2 or T4**.\n\nSimilarly, **Half Flush** requires one suit + honors \u2014 you have W and honors, so possible.\n\nBut T2 and T4 are different suit \u2014 pollute flush.\n\nSo again, better to discard T2 and T4.\n\nThus, **discard one of T2 or T4**.\n\nSince both are equally bad, pick one.\n\nYou have **Play T4** as a valid action.\n\nAlso **Play T2**.\n\nBut earlier you played T4 \u2014 shows intent.\n\nTo avoid hesitation, just pick **T2** \u2014 it\u2019s never been played by you, but that doesn\u2019t matter.\n\nBut actually, you played T4 in history: \u201cPlay T4\u201d appears twice.\n\nSo you\u2019re clearly dumping Bamboo.\n\nBut you also played T2? Not in history.\n\nWait: your history: Play T9, Play T4, Play T3, Play T1 \u2014 you played T1, T3, T4, T9.\n\nOnly T2 remains.\n\nBut you didn\u2019t play T2 yet \u2014 because you only got it later?\n\nAnyway, you have it now.\n\nBut since you\u2019ve been discarding Bamboo systematically, continuing with **Play T2** is fine.\n\nBut **T4 is also in hand and valid to play**.\n\nWait \u2014 your hand: [T2:1, T4:1]\n\nSo you have both.\n\nBut why is **Play T4** available if you already have it?\n\nBecause you can discard any tile.\n\nBut in your valid actions, both **Play T2** and **Play T4** are listed.\n\nSo choose one.\n\nNow, **T4** is higher number, but both are useless.\n\nBut here\u2019s a key insight: **T5 is only 1 left**, and **T6 is 0**, so **T4-T5-T6** impossible.\n\nSimilarly, **T2-T3-T4** impossible because T3=0.\n\nSo no one can use T4 in a chow.\n\nSame for T2.\n\nBut **is T4 more likely to be part of a pung**? Only if someone has two T4 \u2014 but T4 is not a terminal or dragon \u2014 very unlikely.\n\nSame for T2.\n\nSo both are safe.\n\nBut **T2 is a lower number**, sometimes kept for foundation \u2014 but not here.\n\nI think **Play T2** is slightly better because:\n- You already discarded T4 (twice), suggesting you\u2019re down to last one.\n- But you have one T2 \u2014 discard it to clean up.\n\nAlternatively, discard **T4** to be consistent.\n\nBut no strong reason.\n\nHowever, note: **you have W2\u00d72**, and T2 is same number \u2014 but different suit, no synergy.\n\nNo benefit.\n\nBut **F1 and F2** \u2014 discarding them risks someone Peng.\n\nThere is one F1 unshown \u2014 someone might be collecting.\n\nF2 has three unshown \u2014 possible.\n\nWhereas T2 and T4 \u2014 no one can use.\n\nSo **T2 and T4 are safer discards than F1 and F2**.\n\nBetween T2 and T4, **T2 is slightly safer** because:\n- Lower number, less likely to be wanted.\n- But no real difference.\n\nActually, **T4 is closer to middle**, might be slightly more desirable, but with T3 and T6 gone, no.\n\nSo I\u2019ll go with **Play T2**.\n\nBut wait \u2014 your hand has W1, W2, W2, W3 \u2014 if you later draw W4, you could form [W2,W3,W4], freeing up W1.\n\nBut W1 is terminal \u2014 could be useful for Outside Hand.\n\nBut T2 is not.\n\nSo keeping T2 offers nothing.\n\nSame for T4.\n\nBut **T4 is 4**, which is simple \u2014 not helpful.\n\nSo both are bad.\n\nBut let\u2019s check the **valid actions list**: [Play W2; Play W9; Play F2; Play T4; Play W1; Play W8; Play F1; Play T2; Play W7; Play W3]\n\nSo you can play **T2** or **T4**.\n\nNow, **Play T2** removes a dead tile, keeps T4 which is equally dead, but you only need to discard one.\n\nYou will eventually discard both, but now you must pick one.\n\nSince T2 and T4 are symmetric, but **T4 has been played by you before**, it\u2019s consistent to **Play T4** again.\n\nBut you only have one T4 now.\n\nEarlier you played T4 \u2014 so this might be your last one.\n\nBut same for T2.\n\nI think **Play T2** is acceptable.\n\nBut let\u2019s consider **F1**.\n\nF1: East Wind.\n\nPrevailing is West, Seat is West.\n\nNo one has shown strong wind collection.\n\nAcross player has no winds.\n\nPrevious player has F4 pung \u2014 North Wind.\n\nNext player played F1 twice \u2014 maybe dumping.\n\nYou played F1 \u2014 indicates you\u2019re not keeping.\n\nSo **F1 is safe to discard**.\n\nBut still, **T2 is safer** because it\u2019s not an honor.\n\nHonors are always riskier to discard late.\n\nEven if not prevalent, someone might want to complete a pung.\n\nWhereas T2 \u2014 no chow possible \u2014 absolutely safe.\n\nSo **T2 is the safest discard**.\n\nSimilarly T4.\n\nBut **T2 is in the valid actions**, so:\n\nAnswer: Play T2\n\nFinal Decision:\n\nEvaluation: T2 and T4 are dead tiles due to adjacent tiles being exhausted; discarding them avoids polluting potential hands like Outside Hand or Half Flush. F1 and F2 carry slight risk of being punged. W tiles support potential chows in Characters. Among dead tiles, T2 is 